# A program in a terminal pane copies with OSC 52 — neovim's osc52
# clipboard provider, tmux `set-clipboard`, helix, and anything on the
# far side of ssh, where it is the only way to reach the local
# clipboard. The text lands where any copy lands: the unnamed register
# (a `p` in an editor pastes it) and the OS clipboard.
# `terminal.osc52 = false` turns it off; a read is never answered.
#
# The child copies "osc52-payload" (base64 b3NjNTItcGF5bG9hZA==).
write a.txt "\n"
command view.toggle_tree
ex term printf '\033]52;c;b3NjNTItcGF5bG9hZA==\007'; sleep 30
wait 1000
open a.txt
command editor.use_vim
type "p"
expect screen contains "osc52-payload"
